| Node with no right subtree         | Closest greater ancestor       |

## 8. Inorder Successor vs. Inorder Predecessor

This is a common interview comparison.

| Condition            | Inorder Successor             | Inorder Predecessor            |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Meaning              | Next greater value            | Next smaller value             |
| Right subtree exists | Minimum of right subtree      | Not applicable                 |
| Left subtree exists  | Not applicable                | Maximum of left subtree        |
| Otherwise            | Closest greater ancestor      | Closest smaller ancestor       |
| Candidate search     | Greater value → save, go left | Smaller value → save, go right |

Memory trick:

- Successor = next bigger value.
- Predecessor = previous smaller value.
- Successor search: if current node is greater than `k`, save it and go left.
- Predecessor search: if current node is smaller than `k`, save it and go right.

## 9. Interview takeaway

Your solution is the recommended approach for this problem:

```
class Solution {
    public int inOrderSuccessor(Node root, Node k) {
        int ans = -1;

        while (root != null) {
            if (k.data >= root.data) {
                root = root.right;
            } else {
                ans = root.data;
                root = root.left;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
```

Why is it optimal?

1. It uses the BST property to eliminate unnecessary subtrees.
2. It visits only one root-to-leaf path.
3. It does not need recursion, a stack, or an inorder list.
4. It takes \\(O(h)\\) time and \\(O(1)\\) auxiliary space.

One useful detail: your code searches for the smallest value strictly greater than `k.data`. It does not verify that `k` is physically present in the tree. That is fine under the standard GFG assumption that the given node belongs to the BST.

Final pattern to remember:

> Successor = smallest value greater than the given node. Save a greater candidate and continue searching left for a better one.
